          "text": "Welcome to the White Island.\n\nI know what you're thinking — it doesn't look like it should exist. A sandbar of white sand sitting in the middle of the sea, with water so shallow you can walk in it and so clear you can see every grain of sand below you. People always say it looks like the Maldives. I've heard that comparison a hundred times. It's fair.\n\nHere's what this actually is. The White Island is not a real island — it has no rock, no soil, no vegetation. It's a sandbar. A shallow ridge of fine white sand that sits just beneath or at the surface of the water, formed entirely by the movement of sand between the Gulf of Suez and the Gulf of Aqaba.\n\nThis is the exact tip of Sinai — the point where the two gulfs meet. When tidal currents move in different directions from the two sides, they deposit sediment at the meeting point rather than carrying it away. Over time, that sediment builds up into what you see. The shape of the island actually changes. It moves slightly with the seasons, grows or shrinks with strong tidal cycles, and occasionally disappears almost entirely at high tide. Every time you see it, it's technically not quite the same shape it was before.\n\nThe water around it is extraordinarily shallow and warm — in summer it can feel like a bath. And that high salt content we talked about earlier? It makes you more buoyant. Standing in ankle-deep Red Sea water feels like nothing you've experienced in any lake or swimming pool in Europe.\n\nTake your time here. Take your photos. The combination of that white sand against the blue of the Red Sea on one side and the mountains of Sinai on the other — this is one of the genuinely beautiful places on earth. And very few people know it exists."
